 How does Spring Boot manage transactions?
In Spring Boot, transaction management ensures data consistency and integrity — meaning all database operations within a transaction either complete successfully (commit) or roll back completely if any error occurs.

Spring Boot provides declarative transaction management using the
@Transactional annotation, built on top of Spring’s transaction abstraction layer.

Transaction:
A transaction is a set of database operations that must all succeed or fail together.
For example, in a banking application:

You debit ₹1000 from Account A
You credit ₹1000 to Account B
If the credit fails after the debit, data becomes inconsistent — hence both operations should roll back.

How Spring Boot Handles It:
Spring Boot automatically configures transaction management when you use spring-boot-starter-data-jpa or other data starters.

Internally:

Spring uses AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming) proxies around methods annotated with @Transactional.
When a method is called:
A transaction begins.
The method executes.
If successful → transaction commits.
If an exception occurs → transaction rolls back.
Example — Declarative Transaction Management:
@Service
public class BankService {

@Autowired
    private AccountRepository accountRepository;
    @Transactional
    public void transferMoney(Long fromAccountId, Long toAccountId, double amount) {
        Account from = accountRepository.findById(fromAccountId).orElseThrow();
        Account to = accountRepository.findById(toAccountId).orElseThrow();
        from.setBalance(from.getBalance() - amount);
        to.setBalance(to.getBalance() + amount);
        accountRepository.save(from);
        accountRepository.save(to);
        // Simulate exception to test rollback
        if (amount > 1000) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Transfer limit exceeded!");
        }
    }
}
If everything works → transaction commits.
If exception occurs → rollback happens automatically.
Transaction Propagation and Isolation Levels:
You can fine-tune transaction behavior using @Transactional attributes.

@Transactional(
    propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED,
    isolation = Isolation.READ_COMMITTED,
    rollbackFor = Exception.class
)
Propagation:

Defines how transactions behave when calling another transactional method.

REQUIRED: Join existing transaction or create new if none exists
REQUIRES_NEW: Always start a new transaction
MANDATORY: Must run inside an existing transaction
SUPPORTS: Run with or without transaction
NEVER: Must not run inside a transaction
NESTED: Creates nested transaction
Isolation Levels:

Define how transaction handles concurrent access.

READ_UNCOMMITTED: Allows dirty reads
READ_COMMITTED: Prevents dirty reads
REPEATABLE_READ: Prevents non-repeatable reads
SERIALIZABLE: Strictest, ensures full consistency
Programmatic Transaction Management (Optional):
Though rarely needed, you can also manage transactions manually using PlatformTransactionManager.

@Autowired
private PlatformTransactionManager transactionManager;

public void updateData() {
    TransactionStatus status = transactionManager.getTransaction(new DefaultTransactionDefinition());
    try {
        // your database logic
        transactionManager.commit(status);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        transactionManager.rollback(status);
    }
}
Rollback Rules:
By default, Spring rolls back only on unchecked exceptions (RuntimeException and Error), not on checked exceptions (Exception).

You can override this using:

@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)

